#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468ef737-2820-5047-a9db-60f8100c852d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328746} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1489c9-537b-5383-87d8-0caaad2d5cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328747} {:answer/id #uuid "453ed57b-0f79-54c7-8419-74c267e51a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328748} {:answer/id #uuid "39368406-f5e2-5f0f-b061-594f5de5fd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328749} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d1a784-7f6b-5c4a-a244-3ebd63dbabd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453ed57b-0f79-54c7-8419-74c267e51a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328748} :answer-field/id #uuid "155b9131-d31f-566c-8b32-b917dc6f8434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f020700-6aa9-5008-bb57-88129d05abec" :question/math-academy-id "q-86860" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/4772b690cb85fcb4dffd27ac4ba56344307ca106e927b9945144370b266d2be9.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,0]\\times [0,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle III covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
